// Test: bugfixes/loop_outer_string_reassign_no_leak
// Grammar: while_statement, assignment_statement, variable_declaration
// Semantic: SEM020 (accumulator pattern), outer-scope carryover invariant
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.62
//
// Regression: CODEGEN-LOOP-OUTER-STRING-REASSIGN-LEAK
// Fingerprints:
//   - 88dc6aeb0f8e (root-cause diagnostic)
//   - 7289dcf25032 (prior failed-attempt: string_move_and_reclaim)
//   - 5986e77a214f (downstream: WASM-HANDLER-TRAP-JSON-ITERATION)
//
// The canonical shape:
//
//   string item = json.get(arrJson, "0")
//   while item != ""
//       acc = acc + "row-" + item + ";"
//       i = i + 1
//       item = json.get(arrJson, i.toString())
//
// leaks O(N) main-heap bytes because the HIR accumulator-rewrite
// deliberately excludes `item` from the transient arena (it's declared
// OUTSIDE the loop and `mem_release` is a no-op). Every iter allocates
// a fresh string via `__malloc` through `json.get`; the previous iter's
// bytes are orphaned. Cumulative growth trips the 32 MB linear-memory
// cap.
//
// Fix (0.33.62): the HIR builder detects the outer-scope-reassign shape
// and inserts a `carryover_copy(<var>, slot)` re-binding immediately
// after each write of `<var>`. A parity local threads the slot argument
// so successive writes alternate between two fixed 32 KB pools (see
// `src/codegen/native_stdlib/carryover.rs`). The main heap sees O(1)
// live-carryover overhead instead of O(N).
//
// Verification here: 3000 iterations by wrapping around a 10-element
// JSON literal array. Per-iter accumulator append: "row-" + item + ";"
// (4 + 2 + 1 = 7 chars). Total accumulator length: 3000 * 7 = 21000.
// On 0.33.61 (pre-carryover), this pattern trapped at wasm addr
// 0x20xxxxx within ~2000 iters (the leaked json.get results dominated
// the main heap). On 0.33.62 it completes and prints the correct
// length.
//
// A 3000-iteration flat loop is enough to observe the linear-memory
// exhaustion pre-fix while staying well under CI time budgets. The
// index wraps `i = 0` when `i >= 10` so `json.get(arrJson, i.toString())`
// always finds a valid element and the loop only exits when `total`
// reaches 3000.
//
// Expected output:
//   21000

start:
	string arrJson = "[\"r0\",\"r1\",\"r2\",\"r3\",\"r4\",\"r5\",\"r6\",\"r7\",\"r8\",\"r9\"]"
	string acc = ""
	integer i = 0
	string item = json.get(arrJson, "0")
	integer total = 0
	while total < 3000
		acc = acc + "row-" + item + ";"
		i = i + 1
		if i >= 10
			i = 0
		item = json.get(arrJson, i.toString())
		total = total + 1
	printl(acc.length().toString())
